.TH FERROKEY 1 "August 2026" "Ferrokey" "Ferrokey Manual"
.SH NAME
ferrokey \- on-screen keyboard with focus preservation and an embedded terminal workspace
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ferrokey
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-layout\fR \fIid\fR] [\fB\-\-view\fR \fIcompact|full\fR]
[\fB\-\-terminal\fR] [\fB\-\-terminal\-height\fR \fIpx\fR] [\fB\-\-diagnose\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ferrokey
is the Ferrokey on-screen keyboard (OSK). It renders a keyboard over the
focused application without ever stealing keyboard focus, and delivers the
keys the user touches directly to the Linux input core through one
pre-created
.I /dev/uinput
device owned by the sandboxed
.BR ferrokeyd (1)
broker.
.PP
The UI is fully unprivileged. It owns no input devices: every system key
event is sent to
.BR ferrokeyd (1)
over an authenticated AF_UNIX socket using the ferrokey wire protocol; the
broker owns
.IR /dev/uinput .
.PP
In terminal workspace mode
.RB ( \-\-terminal ),
the same keyboard drives an embedded PTY terminal directly \- OSK to PTY \-
with no broker, no
.IR /dev/uinput ,
and no compositor focus involvement.
.PP
The surface backend (Wayland layer-shell, X11 no-input, degraded, or none)
is selected by capability detection at runtime, never by compositor name;
the selection and its reason are logged at startup
.RB ( "surface backend: " \fIname\fR " (" \fIdetail\fR ")" ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-config \fIpath\fR
Load the UI configuration from
.I path
(see
.BR ferrokey.yaml (5)).
Defaults:
.IR /etc/ferrokey/ferrokey.yaml ,
then
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ferrokey/ferrokey.yaml
(or
.IR ~/.config/ferrokey/ferrokey.yaml ).
If none exists, built-in defaults are used.
.TP
.B \-\-layout \fIid\fR
Override the active layout. Accepts a builtin id
.RB ( us ,
.RB de ,
.RB fr ,
.RB gb ,
.RB dvorak ,
.RB us-intl ,
...)
or a system xkb spec
.RB ( us(intl) ,
.RB de@neo ,
...).
The system xkb path requires the xkb feature and libxkbcommon at runtime.
.TP
.B \-\-view \fIcompact|full\fR
Override the keyboard view. Views are visual arrangements over the same
physical-key engine; the view's preferred window size wins over the config
width/height.
.TP
.B \-\-terminal
Start in embedded terminal workspace mode: a PTY pane below the OSK, driven
directly by the keyboard (no ferrokeyd). The child is the configured
terminal shell (default:
.IR $SHELL ,
then
.IR /bin/sh ).
.TP
.B \-\-terminal\-height \fIpx\fR
Terminal pane height in physical pixels (default 420, valid 200\(en4000).
.TP
.B \-\-diagnose
Print terminal-workspace diagnostics and exit without touching the display.
Also accepted as the bare word
.BR diagnose .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B System mode
(default): key events flow OSK to keyboard semantics to the broker to
/dev/uinput to the focused application.
.TP
.B Terminal workspace mode
.RB ( \-\-terminal ):
key events flow OSK to keyboard semantics to the terminal key encoder to the
PTY to the child process. The destination is switched explicitly in the UI;
switching releases all held keys first (a key-down never crosses
destinations).
.TP
.B Diagnostics mode
.RB ( \-\-diagnose ):
prints the terminal engine diagnostics (destination, terminal mode, PTY
status, child state) and exits.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The UI configuration is read from
.BR ferrokey.yaml (5).
Every field is optional; missing fields fall back to defaults.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Wayland session socket name (relative to
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
or an absolute path). Its presence (and the availability of
.B zwlr_layer_shell_v1
) selects the wayland-layer-shell backend.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X11 display. With no Wayland session it selects the x11-no-input backend;
with a Wayland session lacking layer-shell it selects the XWayland fallback.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Runtime directory used for the Wayland socket lookup and the default daemon
socket path.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Search paths for the default config file.
.TP
.B SHELL
The terminal workspace child when the config does not set a shell.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter for the runtime logs (e.g.
.RB info ,
.RB "ferrokey=trace" ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ferrokey/ferrokey.yaml
System-wide UI configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ferrokey/ferrokey.yaml
Per-user UI configuration.
.TP
.I /run/ferrokeyd/ferrokeyd.sock
Default daemon socket (configurable via
.IR socket_path ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including
.B \-\-diagnose
and
.BR \-\-help ).
.TP
.B 1
Configuration error, unknown argument, surface connect failure, or a
runtime error.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On startup the backend selection is logged:
.RB ( "surface backend: " \fIname\fR " (" \fIdetail\fR ")" ).
Degraded modes
.RB ( wayland-degraded ,
.RB none )
print explicit reasons. Use
.B RUST_LOG=debug
for the full startup trace and
.B \-\-diagnose
for the terminal workspace state.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start with the default configuration:
.nf
    ferrokey
.fi
Start in terminal workspace mode with a 600 px pane:
.nf
    ferrokey \-\-terminal \-\-terminal\-height 600
.fi
Use a specific config and layout:
.nf
    ferrokey \-\-config /etc/ferrokey/ferrokey.yaml \-\-layout "de@neo"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ferrokey.yaml (5),
.BR ferrokeyd (1),
.BR ferrokeyd.yaml (5)
